The cycle baseline is `8cc48e46`, whose two narrow-comparison closures remain
provisional. Captured compiler_rt IR md5 is
`b0eda7a6427c3084fdf096a68696f19c`; the stage4 Zig pin is
`bdee02bc0c7f227c0f11bc9a449df673fe803540`. The production and permanent-test
diff has sha256
`915ed4ab53aa1969902964bf2048f837163ec85881201dbbbc988d99cfbc31a4`.

The composed selector now admits only the exact
`llvm.cttz.i64(i64, i1 immarg)` signature with a literal zero-is-poison flag of
zero or one. It materializes the named i64 operand into RDI, reuses the
existing encoded and verified `ctz_i64_rdi_eax` machine operation, and binds
the i64 result in RAX. Other signatures, invalid flags, unsupported values,
and other intrinsics remain fail-closed. The machine, verifier, frame, ABI,
and object-writer authorities are unchanged.

Permanent coverage checks both legal flags, an invalid flag, the exact
machine sequence, composed selection, machine verification, encoding, and
five executable cases: zero, low/high single bits, an interior bit, and
all-ones. The implementation deliberately defines zero as 64 even when the
intrinsic flag permits poison, matching the existing i32 policy.

Fresh report-all advances from the Part 1 state 443 / 250 / 193 to
443 / 256 / 187. Exactly the six expected rows emit provisionally:

- `compiler_rt.floatundidf.__floatundidf`;
- `compiler_rt.floatundisf.__floatundisf`;
- `compiler_rt.floatundihf.__floatundihf`;
- `compiler_rt.floatdidf.__floatdidf`;
- `compiler_rt.floatdisf.__floatdisf`;
- `compiler_rt.floatdihf.__floatdihf`.

A structured extraction containing exactly those six captured bodies emits a
relocation-free ELF object with six global function symbols. A pinned-Zig
`callconv(.c)` executable performs 75 calls against precomputed expected bits:
12 unsigned and 13 signed i64 inputs, each through double, float, and half
rows. Coverage includes zero, both signs, half overflow/rounding boundaries,
the float 24-bit mantissa boundary, the double 53-bit mantissa boundary, and
signed/unsigned i64 extrema. Every bit pattern matches exactly.

